<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Vocontii</h1><p class="st-meta">300 BCE – 124 BCE (approx.) · capital Vasio</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Confederation of the pre-Alps of the Drôme and Vaucluse that became an allied state of Rome, with its towns at Vaison and Die.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Vocontii held the mountains and valleys between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drome">Drôme</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mont-ventoux">Mont Ventoux</a>. They were a league of smaller communities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> campaigned against them under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-fulvius-flaccus-consul-125-bce">Marcus Fulvius Flaccus</a> in 124 BCE, during the first wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Transalpine Gaul</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After submission they kept unusual autonomy as a federated people, with two capitals, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaison-la-romaine">Vasio</a> (Vaison-la-Romaine) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luc-en-diois">Lucus Augusti</a> (Luc-en-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diois">Diois</a>), later replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/die-drome">Dea Augusta</a> (Die). The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompeius-trogus">Pompeius Trogus</a> came from a Vocontian family granted Roman citizenship.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Their land was incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaison-la-romaine">Vaison-la-Romaine</a> preserves one of the largest excavated Roman towns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, with houses, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and a bridge still in use.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 300 BCE – 124 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34